Course.start({"num":28,"unit":6,"slug":"m28-square-roots","minutes":40,"calculator":"allowed for estimates only","title":"Square Roots and Radicals","subtitle":"Square roots, estimating, simplifying radicals, and the Pythagorean theorem","lede":"A square root undoes squaring. Some are whole numbers, like √25 = 5. Most aren't, and their decimals go on forever without repeating. This week: how to work with them exactly.","recap":"Factor by taking out the GCF first. x² + bx + c factors with two numbers that multiply to c and add to b. a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b).","goals":["Find square roots of perfect squares and estimate other square roots","Tell rational from irrational numbers","Simplify square roots, like √50 = 5√2","Use the Pythagorean theorem with exact answers"],"context":{"title":"A homework tablet from 1800 BC","place":"Mesopotamia (today's Iraq) · about 1800 to 1600 BC","text":"In a drawer at Yale University is a small, round clay tablet that fits in your palm. It's called **YBC 7289** (Yale Babylonian Collection, item 7289), and it was probably made by a **student** in ancient Babylon almost **4,000 years ago**.\n\nScratched into the clay is a **square** with its two **diagonals**. Along a diagonal, written in the Babylonians' base-60 number system, is a number that converts to\n\n> **1.41421296…**\n\nThat's the square root of 2, **√2**: the length of the diagonal of a square with sides of 1. The real value is 1.41421356…, so the student's number is correct to about **six decimal places**. Babylonians were doing this calculation more than a thousand years before Pythagoras was born.\n\n√2 has a strange property: its decimals go on **forever without repeating**, so it can't be written as a fraction. Numbers like that are called **irrational**. A legend says a follower of Pythagoras was thrown overboard for proving it. (That part probably isn't true, but the proof is.)","ask":"A square has sides of 1. Why must its diagonal be longer than 1 but shorter than 2?"},"learn":[{"title":"Square roots","text":"The **square root** of a number is a value that, multiplied by itself, gives the number. {{sqrt(49) = 7}} because {{7^2 = 49}}.\n\n- Every positive number has **two** square roots: {{7}} and {{-7}} both square to 49. The symbol {{sqrt(49)}} means the **positive** one; {{-sqrt(49) = -7}}.\n- {{sqrt(-49)}} isn't a real number: no real number squared is negative.\n- Square roots of **perfect squares** (1, 4, 9, 16, 25, …) are whole numbers. Others can be **estimated**: {{sqrt(30)}} is between {{sqrt(25) = 5}} and {{sqrt(36) = 6}}, closer to 5. About 5.5.","check":{"q":"{{sqrt(70)}} is between which two whole numbers?","choices":[["8 and 9",true,"64 < 70 < 81."],["7 and 8",false,"7² = 49 and 8² = 64: 70 is bigger than both."],["35 and 36",false,"A square root isn't half the number."]]}},{"title":"Rational and irrational numbers","text":"A **rational** number can be written as a fraction of integers: 5, −3/4, 0.25, 0.333…. Its decimal either stops or repeats.\n\nAn **irrational** number can't: its decimal goes on forever with no repeating pattern. {{sqrt(2)}}, {{sqrt(3)}}, {{sqrt(5)}} and π are irrational. The square root of any whole number that **isn't** a perfect square is irrational.\n\nBecause we can't write irrational numbers exactly as decimals, we **keep them as radicals**: {{sqrt(2)}} is exact; 1.414 is only an estimate."},{"title":"Simplifying radicals","text":"The product rule: {{sqrt(ab) = sqrt(a) * sqrt(b)}}. To **simplify** a square root, find the **largest perfect square factor**, and take its root out front.","worked":[{"problem":"Simplify {{sqrt(50)}}","steps":[["{{sqrt(25 * 2)}}","25 is the largest perfect square that divides 50."],["{{sqrt(25) * sqrt(2)}}"],["{{5sqrt(2)}}"]],"check":"{{(5sqrt(2))^2 = 25 * 2 = 50}} ✓"},{"problem":"Simplify {{sqrt(72)}}","steps":[["{{sqrt(36 * 2) = 6sqrt(2)}}","36 is the largest perfect square factor. (Using 4 or 9 first also works, but takes more steps.)"]]}],"after":"**Adding radicals** works like combining like terms: {{3sqrt(2) + 5sqrt(2) = 8sqrt(2)}}. But {{sqrt(2) + sqrt(3)}} can't be combined.\n\n**Pythagorean theorem:** in a right triangle with legs {{a}} and {{b}} and hypotenuse {{c}}, {{a^2 + b^2 = c^2}}. With legs 1 and 1: {{c^2 = 2}}, so {{c = sqrt(2)}}: the Babylonian diagonal!","fig":"tri"}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"solve","title":"Square roots","pick":6,"intro":"Type **no real root** if there isn't one.","items":[{"q":"{{sqrt(64)}}","a":"8","from":"sqrt(64)","how":["{{8^2 = 64}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(121)}}","a":"11","from":"sqrt(121)","how":["{{11^2 = 121}}"]},{"q":"{{-sqrt(36)}}","a":"-6","from":"-sqrt(36)","how":["The negative is outside: {{-(6) = -6}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(1/9)}}","a":"1/3","from":"sqrt(1/9)","how":["{{(1/3)^2 = 1/9}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(0.25)}}","a":"0.5","from":"sqrt(0.25)","how":["{{0.5^2 = 0.25}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(-16)}}","a":"no real root","how":["No real number squared is negative."]},{"q":"{{sqrt(400)}}","a":"20","from":"sqrt(400)","how":["{{20^2 = 400}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(9 + 16)}}","a":"5","from":"sqrt(25)","how":["Add first (the root is a grouping symbol): {{sqrt(25) = 5}}"]}],"hints":["Which number times itself gives this?","The root symbol groups everything under it: do the inside first."]},{"id":"t2","type":"select","title":"Estimate","items":["{{sqrt(20)}} is between {4 and 5|3 and 4|5 and 6}.","{{sqrt(50)}} is between {7 and 8|6 and 7|24 and 25}.","{{sqrt(99)}} is closest to {10|9|50}.","{{sqrt(2)}} is about {1.4|1.2|2.1|0.5}.","{{sqrt(10)}} is about {3.2|3.8|5}."],"hints":["Find the perfect squares just below and just above the number."]},{"id":"t3","type":"sort","title":"Rational or irrational?","cats":["Rational","Irrational"],"items":[["{{sqrt(16)}}",0,"= 4"],["{{sqrt(15)}}",1],["{{3/7}}",0],["π",1],["0.121212… (repeating)",0],["{{sqrt(2)}}",1],["{{-8}}",0],["{{sqrt(1/4)}}",0,"= 1/2"],["{{5 + sqrt(3)}}",1,"Rational + irrational is irrational."]],"hints":["Is it the square root of a perfect square (or a fraction of them)? Then it's rational.","A repeating decimal is rational."]},{"id":"t4","type":"solve","title":"Simplify the radical","pick":6,"intro":"Type square roots with the √ key or as **sqrt( )**: **5√2** or **5sqrt(2)**.","items":[{"q":"{{sqrt(50)}}","a":"5sqrt(2)","how":["{{sqrt(25 * 2) = 5sqrt(2)}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(12)}}","a":"2sqrt(3)","how":["{{sqrt(4 * 3) = 2sqrt(3)}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(18)}}","a":"3sqrt(2)","how":["{{sqrt(9 * 2) = 3sqrt(2)}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(45)}}","a":"3sqrt(5)","how":["{{sqrt(9 * 5) = 3sqrt(5)}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(72)}}","a":"6sqrt(2)","how":["{{sqrt(36 * 2) = 6sqrt(2)}}"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(200)}}","a":"10sqrt(2)","how":["{{sqrt(100 * 2) = 10sqrt(2)}}"]},{"q":"{{3sqrt(8)}}","a":"6sqrt(2)","how":["{{sqrt(8) = 2sqrt(2)}}","{{3 * 2sqrt(2) = 6sqrt(2)}}"]},{"q":"{{4sqrt(2) + 7sqrt(2)}}","a":"11sqrt(2)","how":["Like radicals: 4 + 7 = 11"]}],"hints":["Perfect squares to look for: 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100.","Use the biggest perfect square that divides the number."]},{"id":"t5","type":"solve","title":"Pythagorean theorem","pick":4,"intro":"Give exact answers. Simplify radicals.","fig":"tri","items":[{"q":"Legs 6 and 8. Hypotenuse?","a":"10","from":"sqrt(36 + 64)","how":["{{c^2 = 36 + 64 = 100}}","**10**"]},{"q":"Legs 1 and 1. Hypotenuse? (The Babylonian diagonal)","a":"sqrt(2)","from":"sqrt(2)","how":["{{c^2 = 1 + 1 = 2}}","**√2**"]},{"q":"Legs 2 and 4. Hypotenuse?","a":"2sqrt(5)","how":["{{c^2 = 4 + 16 = 20}}","{{sqrt(20) = 2sqrt(5)}}"]},{"q":"Hypotenuse 13, one leg 5. Other leg?","a":"12","from":"sqrt(169 - 25)","how":["{{b^2 = 169 - 25 = 144}}","**12**"]},{"q":"A TV screen is 30 inches wide and 16 inches tall. How long is its diagonal?","a":"34","post":"inches","from":"sqrt(900 + 256)","how":["{{sqrt(900 + 256) = sqrt(1156)}}","**34 inches**"]},{"q":"A square has sides of 5. How long is its diagonal?","a":"5sqrt(2)","how":["{{c^2 = 25 + 25 = 50}}","{{sqrt(50) = 5sqrt(2)}}"]}],"hints":["a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse (c) is across from the right angle.","For a missing leg: b² = c² − a²."]},{"id":"t6","type":"mistake","title":"Radical mistakes","intro":"Tap the **first wrong line**, then type the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Legs 3 and 4: find the hypotenuse.","lines":["{{c = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2)}}","{{c = 3 + 4 = 7}}"],"bad":1,"why":"You can't take the square root of each part separately. {{sqrt(9 + 16) = sqrt(25) = 5}}.","a":"5","from":"sqrt(25)"},{"q":"Simplify {{sqrt(48)}}","lines":["{{sqrt(4 * 12)}}","{{2sqrt(12)}}","Done."],"bad":2,"why":"{{sqrt(12)}} still has a perfect square factor, 4. Use 16: {{sqrt(16 * 3) = 4sqrt(3)}}.","a":"4sqrt(3)"},{"q":"{{sqrt(2) + sqrt(3)}}","lines":["{{sqrt(2 + 3)}}","{{sqrt(5)}}"],"bad":0,"why":"Different radicals can't be added into one root. {{sqrt(2) + sqrt(3)}} is already as simple as it gets (about 3.15, but {{sqrt(5)}} is about 2.24).","a":"sqrt(2) + sqrt(3)"}]}],"build":{"heading":"Find a diagonal","prompt":"Measure (or estimate) the **width and length** of something rectangular: your desk, phone, a door, a sheet of paper, the classroom. Use the **Pythagorean theorem** to find the **diagonal**. Give the **exact** answer (simplified radical if needed) and a **decimal estimate**, and say whether it seems reasonable.","model":"A sheet of paper is 8.5 inches by 11 inches. The diagonal is c, so c^2 = 8.5^2 + 11^2 = 72.25 + 121 = 193.25. So c = √193.25, which is about 13.9 inches. That seems reasonable because the diagonal should be longer than 11 but shorter than 8.5 + 11 = 19.5.","placeholder":"My ___ is ___ by ___. c² = …","reqs":[{"t":"Use a² + b² = c² (with numbers)","re":"c\\s*(\\^\\s*2|²)\\s*="},{"t":"An exact answer with a square root","re":"√|sqrt"},{"t":"A decimal estimate","re":"\\b(about|approximately|≈)\\b|≈"},{"t":"Say whether it's *reasonable*","re":"\\b(reasonable|makes sense|seems)"}]},"reflect":{"q":"A Babylonian student's homework from 4,000 years ago still exists, and it was very accurate. What do you think that student's life was like? What would you want people 4,000 years from now to learn from your work?","placeholder":"I think the student…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Radical operations","items":[{"q":"{{sqrt(12) + sqrt(27)}}","a":"5sqrt(3)","how":["{{2sqrt(3) + 3sqrt(3)}}","**5√3**"]},{"q":"{{sqrt(3) * sqrt(6)}}","a":"3sqrt(2)","how":["{{sqrt(18) = 3sqrt(2)}}"]},{"q":"{{(2sqrt(5))^2}}","a":"20","from":"(2*sqrt(5))^2","how":["{{4 * 5 = 20}}"]},{"q":"{{6/sqrt(3)}} (multiply top and bottom by {{sqrt(3)}})","a":"2sqrt(3)","how":["{{(6sqrt(3))/3 = 2sqrt(3)}}"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"solve","title":"Distance on the coordinate plane","intro":"The distance between two points is the hypotenuse of a right triangle: {{d = sqrt((x₂ - x₁)^2 + (y₂ - y₁)^2)}}.","items":[{"q":"Distance from {{(1, 2)}} to {{(4, 6)}}","a":"5","from":"sqrt(9 + 16)","how":["Across 3, up 4: {{sqrt(9 + 16) = 5}}"]},{"q":"Distance from {{(-2, 1)}} to {{(4, 4)}}","a":"3sqrt(5)","how":["Across 6, up 3: {{sqrt(36 + 9) = sqrt(45) = 3sqrt(5)}}"]},{"q":"Distance from the origin to {{(5, 5)}}","a":"5sqrt(2)","how":["{{sqrt(25 + 25) = sqrt(50) = 5sqrt(2)}}"]}]}],"figs":{"tri":{"x":[0,5],"y":[0,4],"w":220,"pad":24,"bare":true,"segs":[[0,0,4,0,0],[4,0,4,3,0],[0,0,4,3,1],[3.7,0,3.7,0.3,0],[3.7,0.3,4,0.3,0]],"texts":[[2,-0.45,"a"],[4.35,1.5,"b"],[1.7,1.85,"c"]],"alt":"A right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c"}},"culture":"**Did you know?** The Babylonians used base 60, which is why there are 60 minutes in an hour and 360 degrees in a circle. On YBC 7289, √2 is written 1;24,51,10, meaning 1 + 24/60 + 51/3600 + 10/216000.","unitTitle":"Polynomials and Quadratics","code":"OTgyNy1OT0xZQkFC","star":"NjgwNC1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m27-factoring.html","next":"m29-parabolas.html"});
